222625732421875, -0.04486083984375, -0.0005092620849609375, -0.0234527587890625, -0.0292205810546875, 0.049591064453125, -0.006664276123046875, 0.044525146484375, -0.0036678314208984375, 0.0150604248046875, 0.004314422607421875, -0.0703125, -0.0189666748046875, 0.00075197219848632812, 0.047637939453125, -0.0269012451171875, -0.05780029296875, 0.00955963134765625, -0.0281524658203125, 0.0360107421875, 0.0297698974609375, -0.0543212890625, 0.0163421630859375, 0.01486968994140625, -0.007610321044921875, 0.0168304443359375, -0.0517578125, 0.08441162109375, -0.0258636474609375, -0.0305938720703125, -0.045318603515625, -0.06512451171875, -0.012542724609375, -0.02520751953125, 0.00562286376953125, -0.01549530029296875, -0.042388916015625, 0.00018441677093505859, -0.0035076141357421875, -0.0125579833984375, 0.00836944580078125, 0.040740966796875, 0.0276947021484375, -0.0386962890625, 0.07196044921875, 0.0014772415161132812, 0.015960693359375, -0.006969451904296875, -0.018524169921875, 0.01300048828125, 0.0308685302734375, 0.05780029296875, 0.0902099609375, 0.029693603515625, 0.061126708984375, 0.0133819580078125, 0.019256591796875, -0.032562255859375, -0.053375244140625, -0.0594482421875, -0.03826904296875, 0.01235198974609375], `id`: "112a565a-320e-4fa8-9273-dc750b718ddb", `document_type`: "document", `source_document_id`: 1, `text`: "title: Onboarding/1. Search engine overview, body: ﻿Search Engine Overview\r\nhorizontal.app\r\nHorizontal is a search platform that integrates every tool (Drive, Slack, Jira, etc) in a company and provides an awesome search experience for everything. It uses natural language to achieve that.\r\nRepository\r\nThere’s one monorepo that contains three projects:\r\n* api is a Laravel API that handles integrations, indexing, provides the search engine\r\n* graphbuilder is a python app that uses LlamaIndex to build a knowledge graph\r\n* frontend  is the Vue frontend\r\nIndexing\r\nOne of the most important processes of Horizontal is indexing. It refers to the process of retrieving 3rd party data via integrations. Think about downloading files from a customer’s Drive account and storing them in our own database.\r\n\r\n\r\nWe store everything in a Postgres database.\r\n\r\n\r\nThat’s the first step.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe second one is more important.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe system builds a huge knowledge graph in Memgraph.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe graphbuilder project does the heavy work using LlamaIndex and open-source LLMs hosted on FireworksAI.\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\nKnowledge graph (KG) construction\r\nA graph contains nodes and relationships.\r\n\r\n\r\nBuilding a knowledge graph means analyzing sentences and building “triplets.”\r\n\r\n\r\nA triplet means: \r\nsubject -> predicate -> object\r\n\r\n\r\nFor example, if the sentence is “Steve Jobs founded Apple in 1976” then we can construct the following triplet:\r\nSteve Jobs -> founded -> Apple\r\n\r\n\r\nWe can express this as two nodes and one relation:\r\n  \r\n\r\n(usually in a graph we use relations like “founder of” instead of “founded”)\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nIf we add more context, such as: \r\nSteve Jobs founded Apple in 1976. Their first product was Apple I designed by Steve Wozniak.\r\n\r\n\r\nThen the graph would look similar to this:\r\n  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNodes have labels and attributes expressed as JSON. Relations also have attributes.\r\n\r\n\r\nThis is the backbone of Horizontal.\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nThese graphs grow huge over time. This is the graph representation of just 13 (short) Google Docs:\r\n  \r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nSearching\r\nGraph\r\nIn a graph, it’s easy to find related information:\r\n* Find a pivot node based on full-text search (such as Apple)\r\n* Traverse the relationships and explore surrounding nodes\r\n\r\n\r\nBy just traversing through 1 or 2 levels of relationships, we can find out thing like:\r\n* Steve Jobs is the founder of Apple\r\n* Steve Wozniak designed Apple I\r\n* Apple I was the first product\r\n\r\n\r\nThere are great algorithms to traverse nodes, such as BFS (breadth-first search), DFS (depth-first search), Dijkstra’s shortest path, etc.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe problem is that finding the pivot node relies on full-text search (or some variation of that). Which is quite limited.\r\nRAG\r\nRAG stands for Retrieval Augmented Generation.\r\n\r\n\r\nThere are large-language models that are specialized in “embedding.” This means that they are able to create the numeric representation (vector) of any text based on the semantics and meaning.\r\n\r\n\r\nFor example, let’s say, the text “Google Drive integration is an important feature of Horizontal” translates to the following vector: [1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9]\r\n\r\n\r\nThen another text that is semantically similar to this one, will have a “similar” vector. For example, “Horizontal has Jira integration” can translate to [3,4,7,12,8,0,9,-1,5]\r\n\r\n\r\nThese examples are made up, but the two vectors are “similar.” They are close to each other.\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen a user asks a question, we create a vector from it. Let’s say the question is: “what integrations does Horizontal have?”\r\n\r\n\r\nAn example embedding vector would be: [7,3,4,14,-2,8,0,5,-1]\r\n\r\n\r\nBased on these vectors we can run algorithms such as cosine similarity to find similar vectors to the question.\r\n\r\n\r\nOnce we find similar vectors, we can query the corresponding text and send everything to an LLM to answer the question based on the context.\r\n\r\n\r\nSummary:\r\n* The original data is embedded\r\n* User’s question is embedded\r\n* We find similar vectors to the question\r\n* Based on the vector we have the original text as well\r\n* We send everything to an LLM\r\n\r\n\r\nGiven the above examples, the resulting prompt to the LLM would look like this:\r\n\r\n\r\nBased on the given context, answer the question.\r\n\r\n\r\nContext:\r\nGoogle Drive integration is an important feature of Horizontal\r\nHorizontal has Jira integration\r\n\r\n\r\nQuestion\r\nwhat integrations does Horizontal have?\r\n\r\n\r\nIt’s quite powerful because it finds context based on the semantic meaning of words instead of a simple full-text search.", `_node_content`: "{\"id_\": \"112a565a-320e-4fa8-9273-dc750b718ddb\", \"embedding\": null, \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/1. Search engine overview\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 1, \"source_document_id\": 1, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"excluded_embed_metadata_keys\": [\"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"excluded_llm_metadata_keys\": [\"source_type\", \"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"relationships\": {\"1\": {\"node_id\": \"d515d623-1842-494c-b35d-82f8c7fbe2c9\", \"node_type\": \"4\", \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/1. Search engine overview\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 1, \"source_document_id\": 1, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"hash\": \"cb5e3058eb63b09d962b7b345c80edaa15e48612c9bbd638181837ed2e068e65\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}, \"3\": {\"node_id\": \"52ff5c71-ffee-4a01-9bfe-4df437c96476\", \"node_type\": \"1\", \"metadata\": {}, \"hash\": \"dad84ebfdef6e564fd34e98c2e03ac05821c000a76d37f8e27428ac04c71f8fe\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}}, \"metadata_template\": \"{key}: {value}\", \"metadata_separator\": \"\\n\", \"text\": \"\", \"mimetype\": \"text/plain\", \"start_char_idx\": 0, \"end_char_idx\": 4559, \"metadata_seperator\": \"\\n\", \"text_template\": \"{metadata_str}\\n\\n{content}\", \"class_name\": \"TextNode\"}", `_node_type`: "TextNode", `doc_id`: "d515d623-1842-494c-b35d-82f8c7fbe2c9", `document_chunk_id`: 1, `document_id`: "d515d623-1842-494c-b35d-82f8c7fbe2c9", `ref_doc_id`: "d515d623-1842-494c-b35d-82f8c7fbe2c9", `source_type`: "google_drive", `title`: "Onboarding/1. Search engine overview"});
