, -0.034942626953125, 0.026641845703125, -0.01367950439453125, 0.01323699951171875, 0.059600830078125, 0.00475311279296875, 0.035247802734375, -0.0237884521484375, 0.0178375244140625, -0.00890350341796875, -0.01517486572265625, -0.0218505859375, -0.04449462890625, 0.01151275634765625, -0.00981903076171875, -0.051849365234375, 0.0062103271484375, 0.00905609130859375, 0.04388427734375, 0.040618896484375, -0.04608154296875, -0.014556884765625, -0.008544921875, -0.0031375885009765625, -0.0238494873046875, -0.051544189453125, 0.07611083984375, -0.03106689453125, -0.0218505859375, -0.0182342529296875, -0.05755615234375, 0.01203155517578125, -0.046844482421875, -0.0262451171875, 0.0105133056640625, -0.043792724609375, 0.0010194778442382812, -0.01497650146484375, 0.036346435546875, -0.034698486328125, 0.00799560546875, 0.0218505859375, -0.01459503173828125, 0.04913330078125, -0.0177001953125, -0.00971221923828125, 0.00140380859375, -0.0054931640625, 0.003986358642578125, 0.068603515625, 0.048858642578125, 0.06646728515625, 0.021636962890625, 0.02923583984375, 0.021209716796875, 0.01495361328125, -0.0229949951171875, -0.009613037109375, -0.04034423828125, -0.051727294921875, -0.004573822021484375], `id`: "3d612658-aa6d-4c0c-b88b-f9d4f70bef36", `document_type`: "document", `source_document_id`: 3, `text`: "title: Onboarding/5. Multi-tenancy, body: ﻿Multi-tenancy\r\nhorizontal.app\r\nExecutive summary\r\nChange this in your .env file and everything will work:\r\nDB_CONNECTION=central\r\n\r\n\r\nThen run: \r\nphp artisan migrate:fresh -–seed\r\n\r\n\r\nThis will rebuild the new databases and create two tenants called tenant1 and tenant2\r\n\r\n\r\nYou can access their apps at\r\n* tenant1.localhost:9999\r\n* tenant2.localhost:9999\r\n\r\n\r\nThings that you need to do in a different way compared to a non-tenant app:\r\n* Most migrations should be put into migrations/tenant More info here.\r\n* Most routes should be put into routes/tenant More info here.\r\n\r\n\r\nKey aspects of multi-tenancy in Horizontal:\r\n* Multi-database approach\r\n* Subdomain-based tenant identification\r\n* There’s a central and many tenant databases\r\n* Queue jobs are stored in the central database\r\nDatabases in a multitenant environment\r\nHorizontal is a multitenant application.\r\nEach company is a tenant and they will invite their users.\r\n\r\n\r\nOne of the challenges in tenancy is managing the database. There are two main approaches:\r\n* Single database\r\n* Multiple databases\r\n\r\n\r\nSingle database means there’s one huge database with all of the data. Most table contains a tenant key such as team_id, tenant_id, company_id, etc\r\n\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe biggest advantages:\r\n* Fast implementation\r\n\r\n\r\nThe biggest disadvantages:\r\n* Risky. You forget the tenant_id in a delete query and you ruined the data for 10 companies.\r\n* Huge databases. The database will grow huge pretty soon since you store everything in one place.\r\n* Security issues. A random bug in a random query could expose highly sensitive data to another company (tenant 2 sees the data of tenant 1)\r\n\r\n\r\nData is not isolated in this case.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe other option is the multi-database approach. There’s one database server but each tenant has a dedicated database.\r\n\r\n\r\nIn this setup, there’s a need for two kinds of databases:\r\n* Central\r\n* Tenant\r\n\r\n\r\nThe tenant database contains tenant-related data. Each tenant has a dedicated one. This contains documents, integration tokens, users of the tenant, etc.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe central database contains data that is not tenant-related. This data belongs to the application. For example, a list of tenants with database connection information (such as the databases for “Company XYZ” is called “tenant-1234” etc).\r\n  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe central database also contains “admin-related” information, such as admin users who can see internal dashboards (like Horizontal-related dashboards, list of tenants, usage information, etc).\r\n\r\n\r\nMain advantages include:\r\n* Less risk. Whatever you do in one database remains in that database.\r\n* Smaller databases. What was an index before now becomes a dedicated database.\r\n* More secure since data is completely isolated.\r\n\r\n\r\nData is isolated in this case.\r\n\r\n\r\nMain disadvantages are:\r\n* Slower implementation\r\n* Debugging is a bit more complicated since you have to go to the appropriate database first.\r\n\r\n\r\nHorizontal implements the multi-database approach since data isolation and security are orders of magnitudes more important than being a bit faster at the beginning.\r\nTenant identification\r\nThere are a few different ways to identify the current tenant when a request comes in. For example:\r\n* Path such as /api/tenants/asdf-1234/documents\r\n* Header such as X-Tenant-ID: asdf-1234\r\n* Subdomain such as acme.horizontal.app\r\n\r\n\r\nHorizontal uses subdomains to identify tenants. Mainly, because this is the most professional.\r\n\r\n\r\nA new customer comes to the landing page. They click on “Sign up.” Fill out some information. 5 minutes later they’ll have their own Horizontal instance at company.horizontal.app\r\nCode changes\r\nMulti-tenancy requires very few code changes.\r\nRoutes\r\nThere are now two main route files (instead of one):\r\n* api.php\r\n* tenant.php\r\n\r\n\r\ntenant.php contains every route that a tenant uses. Routes such as login, logout, asking questions, etc.\r\n\r\n\r\napi.php contains “central” routes such as internal login, dashboards, etc.\r\n\r\n\r\nTo simplify things, what you would have put into the api.php in a non-tenant application now goes into the tenant.php file.\r\n\r\n\r\nMigrations\r\nNow the application has two kinds of database:\r\n* central\r\n* tenant\r\n\r\n\r\nIt also contains two types of migrations:\r\n* central\r\n* tenant\r\n\r\n\r\n  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEvery table that should live in the tenant database goes into the tenant folder.\r\n\r\n\r\nWhat you would have put into the migrations folder in a non-tenant application now goes into the tenant folder.", `_node_content`: "{\"id_\": \"3d612658-aa6d-4c0c-b88b-f9d4f70bef36\", \"embedding\": null, \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/5. Multi-tenancy\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 4, \"source_document_id\": 3, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"excluded_embed_metadata_keys\": [\"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"excluded_llm_metadata_keys\": [\"source_type\", \"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"relationships\": {\"1\": {\"node_id\": \"feea3810-7cbe-4fb0-a7da-0e4c1ef28abc\", \"node_type\": \"4\", \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/5. Multi-tenancy\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 4, \"source_document_id\": 3, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"hash\": \"03b2de9fcdd964269e962fc0d0b474fe3d84807f51ca967552f7fed24bcfc12a\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}, \"3\": {\"node_id\": \"a4d842a0-c18a-4ebb-991d-de4d2f101343\", \"node_type\": \"1\", \"metadata\": {}, \"hash\": \"37f936ad8254c0c28d978f9f2f706ef4cfadd5f61c07aa782ab55916b5511242\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}}, \"metadata_template\": \"{key}: {value}\", \"metadata_separator\": \"\\n\", \"text\": \"\", \"mimetype\": \"text/plain\", \"start_char_idx\": 0, \"end_char_idx\": 4522, \"metadata_seperator\": \"\\n\", \"text_template\": \"{metadata_str}\\n\\n{content}\", \"class_name\": \"TextNode\"}", `_node_type`: "TextNode", `doc_id`: "feea3810-7cbe-4fb0-a7da-0e4c1ef28abc", `document_chunk_id`: 4, `document_id`: "feea3810-7cbe-4fb0-a7da-0e4c1ef28abc", `ref_doc_id`: "feea3810-7cbe-4fb0-a7da-0e4c1ef28abc", `source_type`: "google_drive", `title`: "Onboarding/5. Multi-tenancy"});
