.0285797119140625, 0.006664276123046875, -0.0147552490234375, -0.00640869140625, 0.04449462890625, -0.0128631591796875, 0.049102783203125, -0.008819580078125, 0.027099609375, 0.00396728515625, -0.0303497314453125, -0.024444580078125, -0.05743408203125, 0.01009368896484375, -0.00804901123046875, -0.058013916015625, -0.01039886474609375, -0.0015630722045898438, 0.01500701904296875, 0.048614501953125, -0.052642822265625, -0.02020263671875, -0.00496673583984375, -0.00061511993408203125, -0.03765869140625, -0.0384521484375, 0.05169677734375, -0.036773681640625, -0.0170745849609375, -0.03900146484375, -0.06805419921875, 0.02044677734375, -0.048431396484375, -0.03607177734375, -0.007785797119140625, -0.025787353515625, 0.0049285888671875, -0.0189361572265625, 0.058135986328125, -0.03436279296875, 0.016082763671875, 0.0068359375, -0.034698486328125, 0.054046630859375, -0.0272979736328125, -0.027740478515625, -0.012420654296875, -0.010284423828125, -0.0133819580078125, 0.06964111328125, 0.058685302734375, 0.0804443359375, 0.03094482421875, 0.0225677490234375, 0.002399444580078125, 0.0237274169921875, -0.023284912109375, -0.0160369873046875, -0.039093017578125, -0.042633056640625, -0.00756072998046875], `id`: "a4d842a0-c18a-4ebb-991d-de4d2f101343", `document_type`: "document", `source_document_id`: 3, `text`: "5 minutes later they’ll have their own Horizontal instance at company.horizontal.app\r\nCode changes\r\nMulti-tenancy requires very few code changes.\r\nRoutes\r\nThere are now two main route files (instead of one):\r\n* api.php\r\n* tenant.php\r\n\r\n\r\ntenant.php contains every route that a tenant uses. Routes such as login, logout, asking questions, etc.\r\n\r\n\r\napi.php contains “central” routes such as internal login, dashboards, etc.\r\n\r\n\r\nTo simplify things, what you would have put into the api.php in a non-tenant application now goes into the tenant.php file.\r\n\r\n\r\nMigrations\r\nNow the application has two kinds of database:\r\n* central\r\n* tenant\r\n\r\n\r\nIt also contains two types of migrations:\r\n* central\r\n* tenant\r\n\r\n\r\n  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEvery table that should live in the tenant database goes into the tenant folder.\r\n\r\n\r\nWhat you would have put into the migrations folder in a non-tenant application now goes into the tenant folder.\r\nDefault database connection\r\nThe default database connection was renamed from pgsql to central\r\n\r\n\r\nThis means you need to change your .env file:\r\nBefore: DB_CONNECTION=pgsql\r\nAfter: DB_CONNECTION=central\r\n\r\n\r\nThis is just a semantic change.\r\nQueues and workers\r\nJobs, batches, and failed jobs are stored in the central database with additional tenant information.\r\n\r\n\r\nThis way, we can run as many workers as we want. Worker processes are tenant-aware and will use the appropriate database when executing jobs.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe infrastructure would become increasingly more complex if jobs were stored in the tenant database. We would need a dedicated worker fleet for each tenant.\r\n\r\n\r\ndocker-compose\r\nThere are two memgraph instances in the local docker compose stack to simulate multi-tenancy:\r\nmemgraph-tenant1:\r\n image: memgraph/memgraph-mage:latest\r\n volumes:\r\n   - memgraph_tenant1_data:/var/lib/memgraph\r\n ports:\r\n   - \"7687:7687\"\r\n   - \"7444:7444\"\r\n command: [ \"--log-level=TRACE\" ]\r\n environment:\r\n   - MEMGRAPH_USER=horizontal\r\n   - MEMGRAPH_PASSWORD=password\r\n\r\n\r\nmemgraph-tenant2:\r\n image: memgraph/memgraph-mage:latest\r\n volumes:\r\n   - memgraph_tenant2_data:/var/lib/memgraph\r\n ports:\r\n   - \"7688:7687\"\r\n   - \"7445:7444\"\r\n command: [ \"--log-level=TRACE\" ]\r\n environment:\r\n   - MEMGRAPH_USER=horizontal\r\n   - MEMGRAPH_PASSWORD=password\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nThe DatabaseSeeder creates two tenants.\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen creating tenants, events are dispatched in the TenantServiceProvider class. One of the events is SetupGraphDBInstance. In local environment it’ll attach one of the memgraph instances to tenants based on tenant names. It’s an easy way to simulate dedicated databases.\r\n\r\n\r\nAt the moment, Memgraph is not ready for production multi-tenancy.\r\n\r\n\r\nThis means, even if you create multiple tenants locally, they’ll use the same Memgraph database. They will overwrite each other’s data.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe reason is that the community edition only allows you to have one database inside one Memgraph instance. \r\n\r\n\r\nThe Enterprise edition costs $25,000 per year. That version supports multi-tenancy 😀\r\nThe problem will be solved in this issue: HOR-58", `_node_content`: "{\"id_\": \"a4d842a0-c18a-4ebb-991d-de4d2f101343\", \"embedding\": null, \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/5. Multi-tenancy\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 4, \"source_document_id\": 3, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"excluded_embed_metadata_keys\": [\"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"excluded_llm_metadata_keys\": [\"source_type\", \"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"relationships\": {\"1\": {\"node_id\": \"feea3810-7cbe-4fb0-a7da-0e4c1ef28abc\", \"node_type\": \"4\", \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/5. Multi-tenancy\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 4, \"source_document_id\": 3, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"hash\": \"03b2de9fcdd964269e962fc0d0b474fe3d84807f51ca967552f7fed24bcfc12a\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}, \"2\": {\"node_id\": \"3d612658-aa6d-4c0c-b88b-f9d4f70bef36\", \"node_type\": \"1\", \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/5. Multi-tenancy\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 4, \"source_document_id\": 3, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"hash\": \"2616e02ea6081d20fb554786075d2046c7036b29266fc10e817c7d5c26b07fb3\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}}, \"metadata_template\": \"{key}: {value}\", \"metadata_separator\": \"\\n\", \"text\": \"\", \"mimetype\": \"text/plain\", \"start_char_idx\": 3605, \"end_char_idx\": 6676, \"metadata_seperator\": \"\\n\", \"text_template\": \"{metadata_str}\\n\\n{content}\", \"class_name\": \"TextNode\"}", `_node_type`: "TextNode", `doc_id`: "feea3810-7cbe-4fb0-a7da-0e4c1ef28abc", `document_chunk_id`: 4, `document_id`: "feea3810-7cbe-4fb0-a7da-0e4c1ef28abc", `ref_doc_id`: "feea3810-7cbe-4fb0-a7da-0e4c1ef28abc", `source_type`: "google_drive", `title`: "Onboarding/5. Multi-tenancy"});
