-0.047882080078125, -0.01959228515625, 0.00019991397857666016, -0.012786865234375, 0.030517578125, -0.0214691162109375, 0.0148162841796875, -0.009124755859375, 0.0248870849609375, -0.0256805419921875, -0.04833984375, -0.02734375, 0.01499176025390625, 0.043853759765625, -0.003490447998046875, -0.059844970703125, -0.0066986083984375, -0.016632080078125, 0.04449462890625, 0.029754638671875, -0.06805419921875, -0.0091094970703125, 0.009765625, -0.03631591796875, -0.003406524658203125, -0.0287017822265625, 0.072021484375, -0.053741455078125, 0.0142669677734375, -0.01837158203125, -0.042694091796875, -0.051544189453125, -0.049591064453125, 0.0245361328125, 0.0079803466796875, -0.042022705078125, 0.0074920654296875, -0.0272216796875, -0.015228271484375, -0.0120391845703125, 0.037353515625, 0.03643798828125, -0.0114593505859375, 0.0455322265625, 0.0186920166015625, -0.0254058837890625, 0.00514984130859375, -0.019012451171875, -0.01090240478515625, 0.04559326171875, 0.048614501953125, 0.0885009765625, 0.032928466796875, 0.0274505615234375, -0.01441192626953125, 0.030517578125, -0.06683349609375, -0.039703369140625, -0.0577392578125, -0.0626220703125, -0.0068817138671875], `id`: "f39c46b2-b1c9-46de-8f39-ffdbeac16023", `document_type`: "document", `source_document_id`: 6, `text`: "title: Onboarding/4. Development philosophy, body: ﻿Development philosophy\r\nhorizontal.app\r\nThe most important thing in the development philosophy of an early-stage product is “ownership mindset.”\r\n\r\n\r\nMeaning, if you have a decision to make, make it. \r\n\r\n\r\nDon’t ask permission to do great things. \r\n\r\n\r\nYou are the owner of this product. You have the permission to make a good decision.\r\n\r\n\r\nWhat makes a good decision?\r\n\r\n\r\nIf the result moves things from 0 to 1.\r\n\r\n\r\nIn other words, if the result serves the purpose of Horizontal, it’s a good decision.\r\n\r\n\r\nWhat’s the purpose of Horizontal?\r\n\r\n\r\nTo be the best search platform for engineering and product development teams.\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen an engineer like you or me first tries it, they should be like “wow. what the hell”\r\n\r\n\r\nThat’s the purpose of Horizontal. That “a-ha” moment.\r\n\r\n\r\nAnything that helps us achieve that is a great decision.\r\n\r\n\r\nHere are two extreme examples:\r\n* “should I put the { character at the end of this line or on a new line?” - no one cares\r\n* “should I refactor this 42-line function into 2 smaller ones?” - no one cares\r\n\r\n\r\nThese things can be important, but not in the early days.\r\n\r\n\r\nFor now, the most important things are:\r\n* Retrieve the most valuable data from customers’ tools\r\n* Find the best data structures, databases, technologies to process information\r\n* Develop the best possible search engine\r\n\r\n\r\nRetrieve. Process. Search.\r\n________________\r\nTechnical guidelines\r\nLong functions over fragmented workflows\r\nThere are some “rules” in the engineering community in regards to how long a function should be.\r\n\r\n\r\nIn my opinion, a function should be as long as it needs to be.\r\n\r\n\r\nIf it’s one coherent workflow then let it be one workflow.\r\n\r\n\r\nFor example, IndexGoogleDrive::handle() is ~120 lines long at the moment. The whole thing is so easy to follow, that I can explain it in 5 minutes to a 16-year old kid by scrolling through the function.\r\n\r\n\r\nIt’s one coherent, but long workflow.\r\n\r\n\r\nThings get fragmented when you try to follow some “rule” and break it into 5 small functions.\r\n\r\n\r\nInstead of line numbers, focus on keeping things simple. For example, the number of indentations:\r\n  \r\n\r\nThis is bad, and should be avoided. The solution is not to extract smaller functions to hide the complexity. The solution is to remove the complexity.\r\nJobs, Services, Actions\r\nPlease use jobs as much as possible.\r\n\r\n\r\nSpinning up a new worker container in production is easy and cheap. It helps us avoid performance bottlenecks and poor UX.\r\n\r\n\r\nIn Horizontal, I started using Services instead of Actions so please do the same.\r\n\r\n\r\nIt’s perfectly fine to have a Service with one function:\r\n* PdfParser has a single stream function\r\n* TextChunker has one public function\r\n\r\n\r\nIf you’re about to build a more complicated service, you can treat it as a “mini application” inside the application:\r\n  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA more complicated service can have its own DTOs, value objects, etc. It can contain subnamespaces.\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nThe main point is that the Jira namespace should contain (almost) everything that Jira needs. No need to navigate to multiple places:\r\n* The DTO is inside app/DTOs\r\n* The Service is inside app/Services…\r\n* Value objects are inside app/ValueObjects\r\n* etc\r\n\r\n\r\nThat’s fragmented and not good in the long term.\r\n\r\n\r\nLet’s aim for self-contained services.\r\nConsistency\r\n“he’s been doing this for over 5 years”\r\n\r\n\r\nIt’s 100% possible to do something in the wrong way for 5 years.\r\n\r\n\r\nJust because a project has a “common practice” or a “pattern” for something it doesn’t mean you need to copy it over and over again.\r\n\r\n\r\nIf it was wrong in the first place, it’s wrong today.\r\n\r\n\r\nConsistency is dogshit if you’re repeating the wrong thing.\r\n\r\n\r\nInstead of blindly following rules, you can just question the “status quo” and come up with great ideas.\r\n\r\n\r\nThis is called innovation.\r\n\r\n\r\nInnovation > consistency\r\nCode duplication\r\n“but we already have this 10-line section in another class”\r\n\r\n\r\nMy go-to rules (which you absolutely don’t have to follow) are:\r\n* If the fix is a new function that contains the 10-line section, go for it\r\n* If the new function would need some boolean arguments and if-else statements, don’t go for it\r\n* If the solution would be a new abstraction (interface, abstract class, trait, some design pattern), don’t go for it\r\n\r\n\r\nThe 2nd and 3rd case usually means it’s too early to make that decision.", `_node_content`: "{\"id_\": \"f39c46b2-b1c9-46de-8f39-ffdbeac16023\", \"embedding\": null, \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/4. Development philosophy\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 8, \"source_document_id\": 6, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"excluded_embed_metadata_keys\": [\"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"excluded_llm_metadata_keys\": [\"source_type\", \"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"relationships\": {\"1\": {\"node_id\": \"1e92795b-3ea3-40e4-a499-d99c1b67daf7\", \"node_type\": \"4\", \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/4. Development philosophy\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 8, \"source_document_id\": 6, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"hash\": \"3ed8090d92bf7fc05964efd67df7880abb7a303f4c4bccfa395b213e28158fc4\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}, \"3\": {\"node_id\": \"ac5f5a95-ce7d-4b81-8d10-de7d5ef60d06\", \"node_type\": \"1\", \"metadata\": {}, \"hash\": \"e99b67a0f9d105979eb45e138e81077098d19d9c5661a01a9f102b76822c1550\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}}, \"metadata_template\": \"{key}: {value}\", \"metadata_separator\": \"\\n\", \"text\": \"\", \"mimetype\": \"text/plain\", \"start_char_idx\": 0, \"end_char_idx\": 4458, \"metadata_seperator\": \"\\n\", \"text_template\": \"{metadata_str}\\n\\n{content}\", \"class_name\": \"TextNode\"}", `_node_type`: "TextNode", `doc_id`: "1e92795b-3ea3-40e4-a499-d99c1b67daf7", `document_chunk_id`: 8, `document_id`: "1e92795b-3ea3-40e4-a499-d99c1b67daf7", `ref_doc_id`: "1e92795b-3ea3-40e4-a499-d99c1b67daf7", `source_type`: "google_drive", `title`: "Onboarding/4. Development philosophy"});
