42431640625, -0.00029754638671875, -0.0140838623046875, -0.00629425048828125, -0.0029659271240234375, -0.0006103515625, 0.03240966796875, -0.0221099853515625, 0.0635986328125, 0.0068817138671875, -0.0192413330078125, -0.0120849609375, -0.042388916015625, -0.0183258056640625, -0.005550384521484375, 0.02105712890625, -0.040008544921875, -0.04852294921875, 0.013702392578125, 0.0028667449951171875, 0.01129150390625, 0.05133056640625, -0.06298828125, -0.00806427001953125, -0.01418304443359375, -0.041595458984375, -0.0020618438720703125, -0.0029277801513671875, 0.037811279296875, -0.040008544921875, -0.003406524658203125, -0.03167724609375, -0.0726318359375, -0.033111572265625, -0.033966064453125, -0.078125, 0.00872039794921875, -0.052032470703125, 0.0112762451171875, 0.0296173095703125, 0.0295257568359375, -0.0220489501953125, 0.06072998046875, 0.0236053466796875, -0.0277252197265625, 0.04827880859375, 0.01251220703125, -0.0236053466796875, -0.0167388916015625, -0.040771484375, 0.0115966796875, 0.02984619140625, 0.0338134765625, 0.08404541015625, 0.05035400390625, 0.0277557373046875, -0.009857177734375, 0.0213470458984375, 0.015869140625, -0.02325439453125, -0.08685302734375, -0.052642822265625, 0.00510406494140625], `id`: "36b08775-79d3-4922-879b-0584983482f4", `document_type`: "document", `source_document_id`: 4, `text`: "title: Onboarding/7. Infrastructure, body: ﻿Infrastructure\r\nhorizontal.app\r\nDatabase\r\nThe problem\r\nWhen adding a database things get a little complicated.\r\n\r\n\r\nRDS databases are private by default. This is for security reasons.\r\nLambda functions are public by default. This is for usability reasons.\r\n\r\n\r\nLambdas can reach out to the internet and because of this they cannot access private resources.\r\n\r\n\r\nSo the core problem is: lambdas cannot reach databases.\r\n\r\n\r\nTo put it into more scientific terms:\r\n  \r\n\r\nThe solution\r\nTo solve this problem we need to build an entire network 🙁\r\n\r\n\r\nIt requires some important concepts.\r\nVirtual Private Cloud (VPC)\r\nIt’s an isolated, private network inside our AWS account:\r\n  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nTo solve the problem we need to move lambdas and databases inside the same VPC.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe config for a VPC looks like this:\r\nVPC:\r\n Type: AWS::EC2::VPC\r\n Properties:\r\n   CidrBlock: 10.0.0.0/16\r\n   EnableDnsHostnames: true\r\n   EnableDnsSupport: true\r\n\r\n\r\nThis defines an IP range for the VPC using the CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) notation. The range 10.0.0.0/16 allows for 65,536 IP addresses, from 10.0.0.0 to 10.0.255.255. This is a common private IP range.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe other two configs make it possible to use hostnames inside the network for better DX.\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nInternet gateway\r\nInternet gateways provide secure access to the internet from a VPC. It is a door between a VPC and the outside world:\r\n  \r\n\r\nThe config is really simple:\r\nInternetGateway:\r\n Type: AWS::EC2::InternetGateway\r\n\r\n\r\nThe next step is to attach the gateway to the VPC:\r\nVPCGatewayAttachment:\r\n Type: AWS::EC2::VPCGatewayAttachment\r\n Properties:\r\n   VpcId: !Ref VPC\r\n   InternetGatewayId: !Ref InternetGateway\r\n\r\n\r\nWith this simple config we already covered:\r\n* Having a secure private network\r\n* Providing secure access to the VPC from the outside world\r\n\r\n\r\nBut things get a bit more complicated from here.\r\n________________\r\nAZs\r\nAWS tries to provide “infinite” scalability and “100%” availability.\r\n\r\n\r\nThey achieve this by having:\r\n* Regions\r\n* Availability zones (AZ)\r\n* Data centers\r\n\r\n\r\nThey have servers in regions such as us-east-1, eu-central-1, etc.\r\nRegions are divided into multiple availability zones.\r\nAvailability zones contain multiple data centers.\r\n  \r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nSubnets\r\nDatabases need to be located at least inside 2 AZs for high availability.\r\n\r\n\r\nWe can spread a VPC across multiple AZs by setting up subnets. Each subnet lives in a different AZ.\r\n  \r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nThe config looks like this:\r\nSubnetA:\r\n Type: AWS::EC2::Subnet\r\n Properties:\r\n   VpcId: !Ref VPC\r\n   CidrBlock: 10.0.1.0/24\r\n   AvailabilityZone: !Select [ 0, !GetAZs \'\' ]\r\n   MapPublicIpOnLaunch: true\r\n\r\n\r\nSubnetB:\r\n Type: AWS::EC2::Subnet\r\n Properties:\r\n   VpcId: !Ref VPC\r\n   CidrBlock: 10.0.2.0/24\r\n   AvailabilityZone: !Select [ 1, !GetAZs \'\' ]\r\n   MapPublicIpOnLaunch: true\r\n\r\n\r\nThey are both part of the same VPC and define a different IP range and AZ.\r\n\r\n\r\nOn top of that the config also needs this part:\r\nvpc:\r\n subnetIds:\r\n   - !Ref SubnetA\r\n   - !Ref SubnetB\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nRoute tables\r\nNow we have components like:\r\n* VPC\r\n* Internet gateway\r\n* Subnets\r\n\r\n\r\nThey are attached, which means they know about each other.\r\n\r\n\r\nNow think about your home network for a moment. Your phone and laptop know about each other. But they still require a router to communicate.\r\n\r\n\r\nConnecting the VPC and the gateway requires a route table to handle. \r\n\r\n\r\nWhen a lambda sends a request to the outside world and gets a response, the router will make sure that the same lambda gets the response:\r\n  \r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nThe config is quite simple:\r\nRouteTable:\r\n Type: AWS::EC2::RouteTable\r\n Properties:\r\n   VpcId: !Ref VPC\r\n\r\n\r\nInternetRoute:\r\n Type: AWS::EC2::Route\r\n Properties:\r\n   RouteTableId: !Ref RouteTable\r\n   DestinationCidrBlock: 0.0.0.0/0\r\n   GatewayId: !Ref InternetGateway\r\n\r\n\r\nIt defines a route table for the VPC and then a connection between the gateway and the route table.", `_node_content`: "{\"id_\": \"36b08775-79d3-4922-879b-0584983482f4\", \"embedding\": null, \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/7. Infrastructure\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 5, \"source_document_id\": 4, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"excluded_embed_metadata_keys\": [\"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"excluded_llm_metadata_keys\": [\"source_type\", \"source_url\", \"document_chunk_id\", \"source_document_id\", \"document_type\"], \"relationships\": {\"1\": {\"node_id\": \"63c57d8e-7c64-4058-a349-7ebc28d0ae7b\", \"node_type\": \"4\", \"metadata\": {\"title\": \"Onboarding/7. Infrastructure\", \"source_type\": \"google_drive\", \"source_url\": null, \"document_chunk_id\": 5, \"source_document_id\": 4, \"document_type\": \"document\"}, \"hash\": \"79fcce26b0fd7ecac61075aa86c3bebf1c4525d684a958b6cc2c39541a32fa41\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}, \"3\": {\"node_id\": \"2c32a83a-e309-42c1-80a5-032a60c480eb\", \"node_type\": \"1\", \"metadata\": {}, \"hash\": \"af2ff079ba718dcf1c7392573a7222b8402240149bdf652f7d154fbad0316475\", \"class_name\": \"RelatedNodeInfo\"}}, \"metadata_template\": \"{key}: {value}\", \"metadata_separator\": \"\\n\", \"text\": \"\", \"mimetype\": \"text/plain\", \"start_char_idx\": 0, \"end_char_idx\": 4043, \"metadata_seperator\": \"\\n\", \"text_template\": \"{metadata_str}\\n\\n{content}\", \"class_name\": \"TextNode\"}", `_node_type`: "TextNode", `doc_id`: "63c57d8e-7c64-4058-a349-7ebc28d0ae7b", `document_chunk_id`: 5, `document_id`: "63c57d8e-7c64-4058-a349-7ebc28d0ae7b", `ref_doc_id`: "63c57d8e-7c64-4058-a349-7ebc28d0ae7b", `source_type`: "google_drive", `title`: "Onboarding/7. Infrastructure"});
